System information\n\nCachyOS Linux, which currently uses GCC 16 by default\n\n### Issue description\n\nGCC 16 (released less than a week ago at the time of this writing) seems to emit more warnings and errors than its previous versions during compilation.\n\n#55157 added a `for` loop that declares an unused count (`j`), which emits an unused variable warning.\n\nAs for #107587, things are more complicated. It seems GCC doesn't like how a definition is done after using a SFINAE. It happens on situations like these:\n\n```cpp\nSTATIC_ASSERT_INCOMPLETE_TYPE(class, Engine);\n//...\n#include \"core/config/engine.h\"\n```\n\nOr:\n\n```cpp\nSTATIC_ASSERT_INCOMPLETE_TYPE(class, RenderingServer);\n//...\n#include \"servers/rendering/rendering_server.h\"\n```\n\nOn a dev editor using GCC, having the include after the assert somehow makes the assert fail by treating the type as being complete, while during compilation, the definition from within the include emits a `sfinae-incomplete` warning.\n\nThere are more warnings and errors in the comments, these are just the ones I could spot right away.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Have any Linux system (or possibly Windows with MinGW) ready to compile Godot (all necessary packages or SDK installed, including SConstruct. GCC version **must** be 16 or equivalent).\n2. Build with `scons`. `werror=yes` helps as compilation will stop at the necessary moments.\n3. Try as much options as possible, such as `optimize=debug` without `dev_build=yes`.\n\nA workaround I have on my machine is to use `CC=gcc-15` when building.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A. It's a compilation issue.","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/119269/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/119269/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}